<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Akova</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1320 CE (approx.) · capital Akova</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Frankish barony in the hills of western Arcadia, centred on the castle the French called Matagrifon, lost to the Byzantines in 1320.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> was among the twelve original baronies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>, created around 1209 and granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-of-rosieres">Walter of Rosieres</a>, with 24 knight's fiefs. Its castle in the mountains between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/elis">Elis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> was called Matagrifon, 'kill the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>', reflecting its role in holding down the region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The barons owed service to the prince and sat in his high court. After the Rosieres line died out, the inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-passavant">Margaret of Passavant</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> was disputed in the 1270s, a famous case recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-romania">Assizes of Romania</a>, and much of the barony passed to the princely domain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1320 the Byzantine governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-asen">Andronikos Asen</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> and other castles of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, bringing the barony into what became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-akova.jpg" alt="Barony of Akova" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: Constantine ✍ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1320 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akova</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walter of Rosieres, Margaret of Passavant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Barony of Matagrifon</dd></dl></aside></div>

